1
00:00:05,405 --> 00:00:10,210
    &l So no one told you
life was gonna be this way &l

2
00:00:10,211 --> 00:00:12,746
    &l Your job's a joke,
        you're broke &l

3
00:00:12,747 --> 00:00:15,815
&l Your love life's D.O.A. &l

4
00:00:15,816 --> 00:00:19,819
   &l It's like you're always
     stuck in second gear &l

5
00:00:19,820 --> 00:00:22,789
    &l When it hasn't been
    your day, your week &l

6
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:25,392
        &l Your month,
    or even your year &l

7
00:00:25,393 --> 00:00:29,029
&l But I'll be there for you &l

8
00:00:29,030 --> 00:00:31,631
&l When the rain starts to pour &l

9
00:00:31,632 --> 00:00:34,034
&l I'll be there for you &l

10
00:00:34,035 --> 00:00:36,636
&l Like I've been there before &l

11
00:00:36,637 --> 00:00:38,772
&l I'll be there for you &l

12
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:42,809
    &l 'Cause you're there
        for me, too. &l

13
00:00:53,887 --> 00:00:57,090
Wow. This place
is beautiful.

14
00:00:57,091 --> 00:01:00,026
            Look at all these
            paleontologists!

15
00:01:00,027 --> 00:01:02,095
    I know. There are going
    to be some pasty folks

16
00:01:02,096 --> 00:01:05,098
    by the pool tomorrow.

17
00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:07,033
            Oh, my God, I can't
            believe you're here.

18
00:01:07,034 --> 00:01:08,268
I think I've been recognized.

19
00:01:08,269 --> 00:01:09,769
This happens all the time.

20
00:01:09,770 --> 00:01:12,972
                Dr. Geller, I am
                such a huge fan.

21
00:01:12,973 --> 00:01:15,175
    That <i>never </i>happens.

22
00:01:15,176 --> 00:01:17,544
        I've been following
        your career for years.

23
00:01:17,545 --> 00:01:19,712
            I can't wait for
            your keynote speech.

24
00:01:19,713 --> 00:01:22,482
            Wow. This is
            very flattering.

25
00:01:22,483 --> 00:01:24,551
                I would love
                your autograph.

26
00:01:24,552 --> 00:01:26,853
Uh... sure.

27
00:01:26,854 --> 00:01:27,887
Um... Dear...

28
00:01:27,888 --> 00:01:28,955
                    Sarah.

29
00:01:28,956 --> 00:01:30,323
    Sarah...

30
00:01:30,324 --> 00:01:34,194
        I... dig you.

31
00:01:34,195 --> 00:01:37,664
                Dr. Ross Geller.

32
00:01:37,665 --> 00:01:38,665
    Oh, thank you so much.

33
00:01:38,666 --> 00:01:39,666
Sure. Oh, and, Sarah,

34
00:01:39,667 --> 00:01:41,067
                I'd like
                to introduce you

35
00:01:41,068 --> 00:01:43,303
            to my colleague,
            Professor Wheeler.

36
00:01:43,304 --> 00:01:44,471
                And this is
                Joey Tribbiani.

37
00:01:44,472 --> 00:01:46,439
Are you a paleontologist?

38
00:01:46,440 --> 00:01:47,774
Oh, God, no, no.

39
00:01:47,775 --> 00:01:49,609
No, no, I'm an actor.

40
00:01:49,610 --> 00:01:50,977
You probably recognize me

41
00:01:50,978 --> 00:01:53,179
 from a little show called
 <i>The Days of Our Lives.</i>

42
00:01:53,180 --> 00:01:55,815
             Dude, it's just
             <i>Days of Our Lives.</i>

43
00:01:55,816 --> 00:01:57,484
            There's no "the."

44
00:01:59,853 --> 00:02:02,989
( <i>chuckling </i>):
Okay, Ross.
Boy, this guy... yeah.

45
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:05,725
No, I play Dr. Drake Ramoray.

46
00:02:05,726 --> 00:02:08,194
I'm sorry, I don't own a TV.

47
00:02:08,195 --> 00:02:10,263
You don't own a TV?

48
00:02:10,264 --> 00:02:13,333
What's all your furniture
pointed at?

49
00:02:15,602 --> 00:02:16,703
All right, I'm going to go

50
00:02:16,704 --> 00:02:18,371
    pick up a few things
        for the trip.

51
00:02:18,372 --> 00:02:19,839
    Oh, I should
    go, too.

52
00:02:19,840 --> 00:02:21,341
Okay. Oh, now, tomorrow--

53
00:02:21,342 --> 00:02:23,109
do you guys want to share a cab
        to the airport,

54
00:02:23,110 --> 00:02:24,611
    or should Mike and I
    just meet you there?

55
00:02:27,914 --> 00:02:31,651
( <i>laughing </i>):
Mike? Who's Mike?

56
00:02:31,652 --> 00:02:34,120
        Mike's your-your
       ex, uh, boyfriend.

57
00:02:34,121 --> 00:02:35,655
    That's right! Oh, yeah.

58
00:02:35,656 --> 00:02:38,591
            Wow. I had totally
            forgotten about him.

59
00:02:38,592 --> 00:02:41,060
            That's a... that's a
            blast from the past.

60
00:02:41,061 --> 00:02:42,662
No, it's okay, it's okay.

61
00:02:42,663 --> 00:02:44,130
        Honest mistake.

62
00:02:44,131 --> 00:02:45,899
    It really doesn't
    mean anything.

63
00:02:45,900 --> 00:02:47,200
    I mean, you know...

64
00:02:47,201 --> 00:02:49,769
Monica refers to Chandler
as Richard all the time.

65
00:02:52,572 --> 00:02:53,806
                She does?

66
00:02:53,807 --> 00:02:55,108
Let's get you out of here.

67
00:03:00,213 --> 00:03:02,782
Well, at least you
took me down with you.

68
00:03:02,783 --> 00:03:03,917
        I'm so sorry.

69
00:03:03,918 --> 00:03:06,252
I just... I keep thinking
        about Mike.

70
00:03:06,253 --> 00:03:07,887
        I'm crazy about David,

71
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:09,989
        and we're having
     so much fun together.

72
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:11,591
    Why-why do I miss Mike?

73
00:03:11,592 --> 00:03:13,960
  That's just... that's-that's
    going to go away, right?

74
00:03:13,961 --> 00:03:16,062
I guess, in time.
                        Yeah.

75
00:03:16,063 --> 00:03:18,932
I mean, my feelings for
Richard are certainly gone.

76
00:03:18,933 --> 00:03:20,466
        You just did it again.

77
00:03:20,467 --> 00:03:23,870
    Chandler-- your feelings
for Chandler are certainly gone.

78
00:03:28,608 --> 00:03:30,977
    You're never going to guess
    who I just saw downstairs.

79
00:03:30,978 --> 00:03:33,546
Oh, uh, Britney Spears.

80
00:03:33,547 --> 00:03:36,449
        Yeah, she never misses
        these conferences.

81
00:03:36,450 --> 00:03:39,219
        No, I just saw
    Dr. Kenneth Schwartz!

82
00:03:39,220 --> 00:03:40,220
Oh, my God!

83
00:03:40,221 --> 00:03:41,221
                I know.

84
00:03:41,222 --> 00:03:42,422
Did you talk to him?

85
00:03:42,423 --> 00:03:45,124
Yeah. What am I going to say
to Kenneth Schwartz?

86
00:03:45,125 --> 00:03:46,659
    You could say, "Hey, Kenny,

87
00:03:46,660 --> 00:03:48,728
            how come you're
            not Britney Spears?"

88
00:03:48,729 --> 00:03:50,196
        You ready to go?

89
00:03:50,197 --> 00:03:51,331
                You-you going
                to go now?

90
00:03:51,332 --> 00:03:52,665
                I thought we
                could hang out.

91
00:03:52,666 --> 00:03:53,967
Oh, I can't.
I have seminars all day,

92
00:03:53,968 --> 00:03:57,437
and I promised Ross
I'd look at his speech.

93
00:03:57,438 --> 00:03:59,839
But maybe we could
have dinner later.

94
00:03:59,840 --> 00:04:01,441
    Out on the balcony?

95
00:04:01,442 --> 00:04:03,309
    It would be romantic.

96
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:05,044
        Will you wear a thong?

97
00:04:05,045 --> 00:04:07,380
    I will if you will.

98
00:04:07,381 --> 00:04:10,583
            You got yourself
            a very weird deal.

99
00:04:13,319 --> 00:04:15,822
        I'm good.
I have dinner plans, so...

100
00:04:15,823 --> 00:04:18,291
So, you going
to be okay?

101
00:04:18,292 --> 00:04:19,392
                    Yeah, yeah.

102
00:04:19,393 --> 00:04:20,827
            I got tons of stuff
            I could do.

103
00:04:20,828 --> 00:04:22,962
I'm going to hit the beach,
go swimming...

104
00:04:22,963 --> 00:04:25,465
            Uh, Joe,
    have you looked outside?

105
00:04:25,466 --> 00:04:27,700
            No. Why?

106
00:04:30,069 --> 00:04:31,504
    Oh, man!

107
00:04:31,505 --> 00:04:32,705
        There's an indoor pool.

108
00:04:32,706 --> 00:04:34,107
                    You could
                    swim there.
                Yeah.

109
00:04:34,108 --> 00:04:35,775
    I wasn't going to swim.

110
00:04:35,776 --> 00:04:37,577
I was going to dig a hole.

111
00:04:41,547 --> 00:04:43,950
        Wow, that "Mike" thing
        was interesting.

112
00:04:43,951 --> 00:04:46,119
            I don't know what's
            going to happen with
            Phoebe and David.

113
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:47,353
        I do.

114
00:04:47,354 --> 00:04:49,589
Want a hint, huh?

115
00:04:49,590 --> 00:04:50,723
        I do.

116
00:04:52,392 --> 00:04:54,694
        I do.

117
00:04:54,695 --> 00:04:58,164
       Okay, I'm sensing this is
          some kind of wordplay,

118
00:04:58,165 --> 00:05:01,601
            because you are pink
    with barely controlled glee.

119
00:05:01,602 --> 00:05:03,436
David's going
to propose to Phoebe.

120
00:05:03,437 --> 00:05:04,637
            What?!

121
00:05:04,638 --> 00:05:05,772
            Why?!

122
00:05:05,773 --> 00:05:07,707
Because we were talking
about ways

123
00:05:07,708 --> 00:05:08,908
that he could beat Mike,

124
00:05:08,909 --> 00:05:11,611
and I told him that Phoebe
wanted to get married.

125
00:05:11,612 --> 00:05:13,880
    Chandler, we have
    talked about this.

126
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:16,482
    You are not supposed
    to give people advice.

127
00:05:16,483 --> 00:05:19,185
Just so I know, what was
so wrong about what I said?

128
00:05:19,186 --> 00:05:21,187
        They've only been going
        out for a few weeks.

129
00:05:21,188 --> 00:05:23,556
      Phoebe is completely
        hung up on Mike.

130
00:05:23,557 --> 00:05:26,426
         She'll say no,
 David's heart will be broken,

131
00:05:26,427 --> 00:05:29,262
   it'll be too hard for them
        to recover from,

132
00:05:29,263 --> 00:05:31,798
  and then Phoebe will end up
          alone again.

133
00:05:31,799 --> 00:05:34,400
Man, that's some bad advice.

134
00:05:41,174 --> 00:05:42,208
            Oh, hey.

135
00:05:42,209 --> 00:05:44,310
Thank God, you guys are here.

136
00:05:44,311 --> 00:05:45,511
    What-what's going on?

137
00:05:45,512 --> 00:05:46,579
                Everything is

138
00:05:46,580 --> 00:05:47,747
    upside down here.

139
00:05:47,748 --> 00:05:51,284
    It rains all day long,
    nobody watches TV,

140
00:05:51,285 --> 00:05:53,119
    and Ross is famous.

141
00:05:55,021 --> 00:05:56,656
All right, I don't want
to alarm anybody,

142
00:05:56,657 --> 00:05:59,092
but Monica's hair is
twice as big as it was
when we landed.

143
00:05:59,093 --> 00:06:02,362
Okay, when I go places
with high humidity,

144
00:06:02,363 --> 00:06:05,098
it gets a little
extra body, okay?

145
00:06:05,099 --> 00:06:09,402
        That's why our
        honeymoon photos look
        like me and Diana Ross.

146
00:06:10,937 --> 00:06:12,705
    Come on, I'll show you guys
    where to check in.

147
00:06:12,706 --> 00:06:15,875
                Oh, honey...
                make sure we get
                a king-size bed.

148
00:06:15,876 --> 00:06:17,577
Oh, David, get one
for us, too.

149
00:06:17,578 --> 00:06:19,812
Oh, and see if they have
a heart-shaped one.

150
00:06:19,813 --> 00:06:23,182
And with mirrors
on the ceiling.

151
00:06:23,183 --> 00:06:25,985
        And make sure our room
        isn't next to theirs.

152
00:06:25,986 --> 00:06:30,223
Oh, you guys are so lucky
you're here with people.

153
00:06:30,224 --> 00:06:32,358
        You know, it's just
        such a romantic place.

154
00:06:32,359 --> 00:06:34,160
            That's all-- I just
            wish I could...

155
00:06:34,161 --> 00:06:36,162
                    share it
                    with a guy.

156
00:06:38,331 --> 00:06:39,399
    Not Joey.

157
00:06:39,400 --> 00:06:40,833
        Not Joey, no.

158
00:06:40,834 --> 00:06:43,569
    I was... I was just
    lusting after Chandler.

159
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:45,304
                    Yeah, right.

160
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:48,708
    So, um...

161
00:06:48,709 --> 00:06:52,011
I'm proposing
to Phoebe tonight.

162
00:06:52,012 --> 00:06:53,546
                Tonight?

163
00:06:53,547 --> 00:06:57,617
    Isn't an engagement ring
    supposed to have a diamond?

164
00:06:57,618 --> 00:07:00,486
                Oh, there it is.

165
00:07:00,487 --> 00:07:02,722
Yes, well, being
a failed scientist

166
00:07:02,723 --> 00:07:05,291
doesn't pay quite as well
as you might think.

167
00:07:05,292 --> 00:07:08,227
That's um, that's
1/70 of a karat.

168
00:07:08,228 --> 00:07:10,163
And the clarity is...

169
00:07:10,164 --> 00:07:11,898
is quite poor.

170
00:07:11,899 --> 00:07:13,833
                    Nice.

171
00:07:15,568 --> 00:07:17,437
Monica, can I talk to you
for a second?

172
00:07:17,438 --> 00:07:19,505
                    Okay.

173
00:07:19,506 --> 00:07:21,307
David's going to propose
to Phoebe tonight.

174
00:07:21,308 --> 00:07:23,309
See what happens when you
    give people advice?

175
00:07:24,777 --> 00:07:26,312
    I hope you told him not to.

176
00:07:26,313 --> 00:07:28,114
That would be advice!

177
00:07:28,115 --> 00:07:31,517
        Okay, fine.
    I'll handle this.

178
00:07:33,653 --> 00:07:34,854
    Phoebe.

179
00:07:34,855 --> 00:07:35,855
                    Yes?

180
00:07:35,856 --> 00:07:37,723
    I need to talk to you.

181
00:07:37,724 --> 00:07:40,126
Are you leaving
the Supremes?

182
00:07:45,565 --> 00:07:46,766
                Okay, my husband

183
00:07:46,767 --> 00:07:50,470
        just gave your boyfriend
        some very bad advice.

184
00:07:50,471 --> 00:07:53,372
        David's going to propose
        to you tonight.

185
00:07:53,373 --> 00:07:56,642
    Wow. Really?

186
00:07:56,643 --> 00:07:59,512
That's fantastic.

187
00:07:59,513 --> 00:08:01,047
        What? Are you serious?

188
00:08:01,048 --> 00:08:02,081
        You want to marry him?

189
00:08:02,082 --> 00:08:03,216
                What about Mike?

190
00:08:03,217 --> 00:08:05,284
Oh, okay, you want me
to marry Mike?

191
00:08:05,285 --> 00:08:07,720
All right, well, let's just
gag him and handcuff him

192
00:08:07,721 --> 00:08:09,388
and force him down
the aisle, okay?

193
00:08:09,389 --> 00:08:10,756
I mean, I can just see it.

194
00:08:10,757 --> 00:08:12,124
"Mike, do you take Phoebe?"

195
00:08:12,125 --> 00:08:13,326
( <i>muffled protest </i>)

196
00:08:14,827 --> 00:08:17,930
You know, it's
every girl's dream.

197
00:08:17,931 --> 00:08:20,366
    Do you really think
    that marrying someone else

198
00:08:20,367 --> 00:08:21,501
            is the right answer?

199
00:08:21,502 --> 00:08:22,668
        Sure.

200
00:08:22,669 --> 00:08:25,605
Look, okay, bottom line--
I love Mike... David!

201
00:08:25,606 --> 00:08:27,540
Uh... David.

202
00:08:27,541 --> 00:08:29,942
    I love David.

203
00:08:29,943 --> 00:08:32,044
Don't look at me that way,
Roseanne Roseannadanna.

204
00:08:35,615 --> 00:08:38,885
"By using CT scans
and computer imaging,

205
00:08:38,886 --> 00:08:42,188
"we can, in a very real way,
bring the Mesozoic Era

206
00:08:42,189 --> 00:08:46,526
into the 21st century."

207
00:08:46,527 --> 00:08:48,427
                    It's great.

208
00:08:48,428 --> 00:08:50,296
        You're going to be the
        hit of the conference.

209
00:08:50,297 --> 00:08:52,665
    Oh, and you know what?
It'll be even better tomorrow

210
00:08:52,666 --> 00:08:54,901
because I won't be constantly
    interrupted by Joey

211
00:08:54,902 --> 00:08:57,503
checking to see if they put
chocolates on my pillow yet.

212
00:08:57,504 --> 00:08:59,171
        ( <i>knocking </i>)

213
00:09:00,773 --> 00:09:01,774
Hey, guys.

214
00:09:01,775 --> 00:09:03,509
                The chocolates
                aren't here yet.

215
00:09:03,510 --> 00:09:05,278
    Damn it!

216
00:09:05,279 --> 00:09:06,612
            Ross just read me
            his speech.

217
00:09:06,613 --> 00:09:07,613
                It's fantastic.

218
00:09:07,614 --> 00:09:09,382
Oh, is it on the computer?

219
00:09:09,383 --> 00:09:11,551
'Cause I'd love
to give it a read.

220
00:09:11,552 --> 00:09:14,487
        If you want to check
        your e-mail, just ask.

221
00:09:14,488 --> 00:09:15,955
    What? May I?

222
00:09:18,391 --> 00:09:20,760
What's with the rain, Geller?

223
00:09:20,761 --> 00:09:23,329
    I mean, when I signed up
        for dino week,

224
00:09:23,330 --> 00:09:25,998
    nobody said anything
about it being monsoon season.

225
00:09:25,999 --> 00:09:28,200
Actually, the wet season
is June to December.

226
00:09:28,201 --> 00:09:30,870
It's not the time, Charlie.

227
00:09:30,871 --> 00:09:32,505
No, no! Dear God, no!

228
00:09:32,506 --> 00:09:36,108
            Oh, what, did
            someone outbid you
            for the teapot?

229
00:09:37,410 --> 00:09:39,912
        Secret teapot?

230
00:09:39,913 --> 00:09:41,681
Your computer,
I don't know wha...

231
00:09:41,682 --> 00:09:43,049
Everything's gone.

232
00:09:43,050 --> 00:09:44,050
    What do you mean?

233
00:09:44,051 --> 00:09:45,117
            It must be a virus.

234
00:09:45,118 --> 00:09:47,019
            I think it erased
            your hard drive.

235
00:09:47,020 --> 00:09:48,287
            What?

236
00:09:48,288 --> 00:09:50,356
Oh, my God.
What did you do?

237
00:09:50,357 --> 00:09:52,558
            Someone I don't know
            sent me an e-mail,
            and I opened it.

238
00:09:52,559 --> 00:09:53,659
Why? Why would you open it?

239
00:09:53,660 --> 00:09:55,628
    Well, it didn't say
    "This is a virus."

240
00:09:55,629 --> 00:09:58,364
What <i>did </i>it say?

241
00:09:58,365 --> 00:09:59,999
                    "Nude...

242
00:10:01,367 --> 00:10:03,636
pictures of Anna Kournikova."

243
00:10:03,637 --> 00:10:06,539
                I'm so sorry.

244
00:10:06,540 --> 00:10:08,574
     What am I going to do?
  My speech is gone, Chandler.

245
00:10:08,575 --> 00:10:09,709
                It's not gone.

246
00:10:09,710 --> 00:10:11,077
            I mean, I'm sure you
            printed out a copy.

247
00:10:11,078 --> 00:10:12,078
    You have a hard copy, right?

248
00:10:12,079 --> 00:10:13,479
        No, I don't!

249
00:10:13,480 --> 00:10:15,448
            Well, you must be
            pretty mad

